#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Put a release's own pieces on this Rex host, as the desktop does when
it connects after an upgrade: Olo's helper (`olo-rex-link`, which is
`olo agent` and the agents service), the skill for agents with its `olo`
command, and the desktop commands' `olo`. For a host whose desktop can't
be upgraded just now (Ben, 2026-11-26: "as you build the new releases
please upgrade the services, skills etc on this rex host automatically
for me so that the agents working on stuff relying on olo agent fixes
etc get it as soon as they're ready (i'll be stepping away for a while
and won't be able to upgrade my desktop for a bit)").

    tools/dev/host-upgrade [--dry-run] [--work DIR] [--even-if-held] [--under-screens] [--keep-service]

DIR: where the release was built (tools/publish-release.sh's work folder:
/var/tmp/olo-release, or OLO_RELEASE_WORK). tools/publish-release.sh runs
this as its last step, once the release is published (OLO_NO_HOST_UPGRADE=1
has it leave the host alone); run by hand, it installs what was last
built there. Only on a host that has Olo's pieces already (its helper's
folder): it upgrades, it never sets a host up.

What it does, each as the desktop's own install does it
(apps/rex/src/bridge.rs, link.rs, build.rs):

1. The helper the release's Rex carries for this machine, under the
   name that Rex will look for (`olo-rex-link-ID-ARCH` in
   ~/.local/share/olo/rex-link: ID is the app's own, of both machines'
   helpers), so the desktop finds it there when it is upgraded and puts
   nothing again. The newest by its time, which is the one `olo` runs.
2. The skill's files (~/.claude/skills/olo-rex-bridge: SKILL.md, olo,
   olo.lua) and the desktop commands' own `olo` (~/.local/share/olo/
   rex-commands), from the release's source. Their stamps (`.olo-skill`,
   `.olo-commands`) are left as the desktop wrote them: a desktop of an
   earlier release that connects meanwhile sees its own stamp and leaves
   the newer files be, and the upgraded desktop sees a stamp that isn't
   its own and writes its own once.
3. The agents service: the one running is asked to retire (it finishes
   what it is doing and writes down what it knows; the children are the
   Rex server's tabs and go on, and a `watch` in flight is asked again by
   its own command), and the new helper's starts. From 2026-11-26 a
   service hands over by itself to an asker of a later build; one from
   before must be told. From 2026-11-27 the service that retires starts
   the new helper's itself, its lock handed down (`retire --to HELPER`):
   one step, whoever else asks meanwhile. One from before that just
   ends, and an asker of an old build may start the old one again in
   that moment: this tool takes such a service over in its place
   (`take_over`: a child of the tool sleeps on the service's lock and
   becomes the new helper's service the moment the old one's process
   ends). Where neither can be done it is asked the old way, until the
   service is the new helper's. The running service is found without
   asking it through the new helper (`quietly`): asked so, a service
   that isn't held hands itself over the old way.

4. Olo itself, where this host has it for its agents' screens
   (~/.local/share/olo/desktop: `olo screen install` put it there):
   brought to this release from the release's own archive, by the new
   helper (`screen install --from`: only the programs that differ are
   written, each renamed into place). Never put on a host that hasn't
   it, and not while a screen runs there: a screen follows the Olo it
   runs from (its app_server reloads in place a moment after the
   programs change), which is nothing to do to an agent in the middle
   of a page; it says which run, and is done at the next upgrade that
   finds none (`--under-screens`: all the same).

A hold: while ~/.local/state/olo/host-upgrade.hold is there, nothing is
replaced (its words say why and whose it is); --even-if-held goes ahead
all the same. Whoever has agents at work on the host may put it there.
The agents service keeps to it too (2026-11-26): while it is there a
running service is handed to no later build of the helper (a desktop
that upgraded and connected is answered by the one that runs) and
`olo-rex-link agent retire` is refused without `--even-if-held`.

--service-only HELPER: the service alone is handed to a helper that is
on the host already (its whole path); nothing is put. A hold holds it
too, without `--even-if-held`.

--keep-service: the helper, the skill and the commands are put and the
agents service that runs is left running (asked nothing). For a release
whose helper changes only what the `olo agent` command itself does
(`run`, `watch --follow`: several askings of the service, made by the
command), which a service of the same protocol answers as it is. It
goes ahead where a hold is there too: nothing a hold is for is touched.
On a host whose service doesn't keep to a hold yet (before update 30)
there must be a hold or none of this: a later helper asking is what
hands such a service over.

It prints what it did, and its last line is "host-upgrade: done: …",
"host-upgrade: nothing to do: …" or "host-upgrade: failed: WHY" (exit 1).
"""
import fcntl
import glob
import json
import os
import platform
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
LINKS = os.path.join(HOME, ".local/share/olo/rex-link")
SKILL = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude/skills/olo-rex-bridge")
COMMANDS = os.path.join(HOME, ".local/share/olo/rex-commands")
HOLD = os.path.join(HOME, ".local/state/olo/host-upgrade.hold")
# (The order the app's build.rs takes them in for its ID.)
MACHINES = ["x86_64", "aarch64"]


def fail(why):
    print(f"host-upgrade: failed: {why}")
    sys.exit(1)


def fnv(parts):
    """FNV-1a over bytes, as apps/rex/build.rs names the helper's build
    and bridge.rs stamps the skill."""
    h = 0xCBF29CE484222325
    for part in parts:
        for byte in part:
            h = ((h ^ byte) * 0x100000001B3) & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
    return f"{h:016x}"


def put(path, data, mode, dry):
    """`data` at `path`, whole or not at all; whether it changed."""
    try:
        if open(path, "rb").read() == data and (os.stat(path).st_mode & 0o777) == mode:
            return False
    except OSError:
        pass
    if not dry:
        tmp = f"{path}.tmp.{os.getpid()}"
        with open(tmp, "wb") as f:
            f.write(data)
        os.chmod(tmp, mode)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    return True


def asked(helper, *args, timeout=60):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([helper, "agent", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        return None, str(e)
    try:
        return json.loads(r.stdout), r.stderr.strip()
    except ValueError:
        return None, (r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()


def running(pid):
    """Whether a process still runs. One that has ended and whose parent
    hasn't collected it is still in /proc (a service started by a watch
    of an older build, which never collects it): each asking of the
    06:15 handover waited its whole thirty seconds on one."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            return f.read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[0] != "Z"
    except (OSError, IndexError):
        return False


def quietly(helper):
    """The running service `helper`'s would take over, found without
    disturbing it: (pid, its file, whether it starts its successor
    itself), or (None, why, False) where none answers. A service hands
    itself to the first asker of a *later* build, and that is the step
    this tool means to take with care, not by asking; and the oldest
    helper kept on a host may be from before there was a service at
    all. So: the services run from a file beside `helper` (as a service
    itself judges who may take it over), for the Rex server asked of
    here where one is named; and the one that serves among them (the
    others wait for its lock a moment and go) is asked for through the
    oldest of *their* files, which any of them answers as it is."""
    beside, want = os.path.dirname(helper), os.environ.get("REX_SERVER_SOCKET")
    found = {}
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            if os.stat(f"/proc/{entry}").st_uid != os.getuid():
                continue
            cmd = open(f"/proc/{entry}/cmdline", "rb").read().split(b"\0")
            if cmd[1:3] != [b"agent", b"--server"] or cmd[4:5] != [b"serve"] or (want and os.fsdecode(cmd[3]) != want):
                continue
            exe = os.readlink(f"/proc/{entry}/exe").removesuffix(" (deleted)")
            if os.path.dirname(exe) == beside and running(entry) and os.path.isfile(exe):
                found[int(entry)] = exe
        except (OSError, IndexError):
            continue
    if not found:
        return None, "no agents service is running", False
    said, err = asked(min(found.values(), key=os.path.getmtime), "ping")
    if not said or said.get("pid") not in found:
        return None, err or "none answers yet", False
    return said["pid"], found[said["pid"]], bool(said.get("hands_over"))


def take_over(helper, pid, flags):
    """One step for a service from before services started their own
    successors (no `hands_over` in its `ping`): this tool plays the
    retiring service's part. Such a service just ends, and the next is
    whichever build takes its lock first: the services that askers of
    older builds start look for the lock every 20 ms, and on a host with
    thirty watches one of theirs nearly always has it before a service
    this tool then asks for. So a child of this tool waits for that lock
    *asleep in the kernel* (a blocking `flock`: woken the moment it is
    released) and, the lock its own, **becomes** the new helper's
    service (an exec, the lock's descriptor handed down as a retiring
    service hands it: `OLO_REX_AGENTS_LOCK_FD`), in a session of its
    own, with the old service's environment and its log; the tool then
    asks the service to retire. The lock is released only when the old
    service's process ends, its record written: the two never overlap.
    One of the lookers can still get in between the release and the wake
    (a few in a hundred): that one is retired too, and the child sleeps
    on. Returns (whether the new helper's service runs, what to say)."""
    try:
        cmdline = open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb").read().split(b"\0")
        server = cmdline[cmdline.index(b"--server") + 1]
        if b"serve" not in cmdline or b"agent" not in cmdline:
            return False, f"pid {pid} isn't an agents service"
        env = dict(e.split(b"=", 1) for e in open(f"/proc/{pid}/environ", "rb").read().split(b"\0") if b"=" in e)
        lock = next(t for t in (os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/fd/{fd}") for fd in os.listdir(f"/proc/{pid}/fd")) if t.endswith("/lock"))
        log = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/fd/1")
    except (OSError, ValueError, StopIteration) as e:
        return False, f"what pid {pid} runs with couldn't be read ({e or 'no lock among its files'})"
    if not log.endswith("/service.log"):
        return False, f"pid {pid}'s log isn't a service's ({log})"
    env.pop(b"OLO_REX_AGENTS_LOCK_FD", None)
    sys.stdout.flush()
    child = os.fork()
    if child == 0:
        try:
            os.setsid()
            held = os.open(lock, os.O_WRONLY)
            fcntl.flock(held, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
            os.set_inheritable(held, True)
            out = os.open(log, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND)
            os.dup2(os.open("/dev/null", os.O_RDONLY), 0)
            os.dup2(out, 1)
            os.dup2(out, 2)
            os.chdir("/")
            env[b"OLO_REX_AGENTS_LOCK_FD"] = str(held).encode()
            os.execve(helper, [helper, "agent", "--server", server, "serve"], env)
        finally:
            os._exit(127)

    def asleep():
        # (The kernel's own list: a waiter is a line with "->" and its pid.)
        try:
            return any("->" in line and f" {child} " in line for line in open("/proc/locks"))
        except OSError:
            return False

    until = time.time() + 3
    while not asleep() and time.time() < until:
        time.sleep(0.01)
    retired, until = {pid}, time.time() + 60
    _, refused = asked(helper, "retire", *flags)
    if "held" in (refused or ""):
        # (It keeps to a hold this tool wasn't told to pass: the child
        # is this tool's own process still, asleep on the lock.)
        os.kill(child, 9)
        os.waitpid(child, 0)
        return False, "the service is held at the build it runs"
    while True:
        now, runs, _ = quietly(helper)
        if runs == helper:
            between = len(retired) - 1
            return True, "in one step" if not between else f"in one step but for {between} service{'s' if between > 1 else ''} of an older build that got in between and was retired too"
        # (One that got in between: a service of an older build, retired like the first.)
        if now is not None and now not in retired:
            if len(retired) >= 6:
                break
            retired.add(now)
            asked(helper, "retire", *flags)
        if time.time() > until or not os.path.exists(f"/proc/{child}"):
            break
        time.sleep(0.05)
    # Given up: the child, if it still sleeps (it is this tool's own
    # process until it has the lock; once it is the service it stays).
    try:
        if os.readlink(f"/proc/{child}/exe") != helper:
            os.kill(child, 9)
            os.waitpid(child, 0)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return False, f"the new helper's service didn't begin within a minute ({len(retired)} service{'s' if len(retired) > 1 else ''} retired)"


def service(helper):
    """The running service's process and the file it runs, asked through
    `helper` (which starts one if none runs): (pid, exe) or (None, why)."""
    said, err = asked(helper, "ping")
    if not said or "pid" not in said:
        # (None answers just now, whatever `ping` said of it: it starts
        # none, and a service that has just handed over to this very
        # asking is gone by the time the answer is read. A command that
        # starts one starts this helper's, so the new build is the one
        # running from this moment, not whichever an asker of an old
        # build starts first, and nobody waits a second for a service
        # (the upgrades of 09:43 and 10:50, 2026-11-26).)
        asked(helper, "list", "--fast", "--json")
        said, err = asked(helper, "ping")
    if not said or "pid" not in said:
        return None, err or "no answer"
    pid = int(said["pid"])
    try:
        return pid, os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/exe").removesuffix(" (deleted)")
    except OSError as e:
        return None, str(e)


def hand_over(helper, args, keep_service):
    """The service handed to `helper`'s. (What was done, or None; what
    was left as it is, or None.)"""
    did, kept = None, None
    # One step where it can be: a service that starts its successor is
    # told which (`retire --to`); one from before is taken over by this
    # tool itself (`take_over`). Asked quietly first: a service hands
    # itself to the first asker of a later build, the old way.
    took = None
    if not keep_service:
        was, runs, hands_over = quietly(helper)
        flags = ["--even-if-held"] if "--even-if-held" in args else []
        if was is not None and runs != helper:
            if hands_over:
                asked(helper, "retire", "--to", helper, *flags)
                how = "in one step"
            else:
                ok, how = take_over(helper, was, flags)
                if not ok:
                    print(f"host-upgrade: the service couldn't be taken over in one step ({how}): asked the old way")
            until = time.time() + 30
            while time.time() < until and quietly(helper)[1] != helper:
                time.sleep(0.05)
            now, runs, _ = quietly(helper)
            if runs == helper:
                took = f"the agents service (pid {was} retired, pid {now} is the new helper's, {how})"
    pid, exe = service(helper)
    if took and exe == helper:
        did = took
    for attempt in range(1, 13):
        # (Left as it is: asked which it is, which a service that keeps
        # to its hold answers for a later helper without handing over.)
        if keep_service:
            if pid is not None and exe != helper:
                kept = f"the agents service left as it is (pid {pid}, {os.path.basename(exe)})"
            break
        if pid is None:
            # (No Rex server here, or it can't be asked: nothing runs to be upgraded.)
            print(f"host-upgrade: the agents service couldn't be asked ({exe}): it starts from the new helper at the next `olo agent`")
            break
        if exe == helper:
            break
        was = pid
        # (A service that is held keeps to the hold itself: it is ended
        # only by one who says they know of it, as this was told to be.)
        # (`--to`: a service that knows the word starts the new helper's
        # itself as it ends, its lock handed down, so no asker of an
        # older build can start theirs in between: one step. One from
        # before the word just ends, and whoever asks first starts the
        # next: the handover of 06:15, 2026-11-27, took six of these.)
        asked(helper, "retire", "--to", helper, *(["--even-if-held"] if "--even-if-held" in args else []))
        until = time.time() + 30
        while running(was) and time.time() < until:
            time.sleep(0.05)
        pid, exe = service(helper)
        if exe == helper:
            did = f"the agents service (pid {was} retired, pid {pid} is the new helper's" + (f", asked {attempt} times" if attempt > 1 else "") + ")"
            break
    else:
        fail(f"the agents service is still pid {pid} running {exe} after 12 askings: an asker of the old build starts it again each time; `{helper} agent retire` and look")
    return did, kept


def main():
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    # The service alone, to a helper that is on the host already (its
    # whole path): nothing put, nothing else touched. Also how this step
    # is tried in a scratch place (crates/rex-link/tests/agents.rs).
    if "--service-only" in args:
        helper = os.path.abspath(args[args.index("--service-only") + 1])
        if not os.access(helper, os.X_OK):
            fail(f"{helper} isn't a helper that can be run")
        if os.path.exists(HOLD) and "--even-if-held" not in args:
            why = " ".join(open(HOLD).read().split())[:200]
            print(f"host-upgrade: nothing to do: held by {HOLD} ({why or 'no word of why'}): remove it, or --even-if-held")
            return
        handed, _ = hand_over(helper, args, False)
        print(f"host-upgrade: done: {handed}" if handed else f"host-upgrade: nothing to do: the agents service runs {os.path.basename(helper)} already, or none runs")
        return
    dry = "--dry-run" in args
    work = os.environ.get("OLO_RELEASE_WORK", "/var/tmp/olo-release")
    if "--work" in args:
        work = args[args.index("--work") + 1]
    if not os.path.isdir(LINKS):
        print(f"host-upgrade: nothing to do: this host has none of Olo's helper ({LINKS}); the desktop sets a host up")
        return
    # Held: whoever has agents at work on this host says so with a file,
    # and nothing here is replaced while it is there (its words are why).
    # (2026-11-26: a service replaced under four agents hours into their
    # work, for 2 min 21 s, by a build of mine; their lead asked to be
    # told before the service is replaced. A hold is how they say no.)
    keep_service = "--keep-service" in args
    kept = None
    # (A service is handed to a later helper that asks it, where no hold
    # is there for it to keep to: with none, putting the helper alone
    # would replace the service all the same, at the next asking.)
    if keep_service and not os.path.exists(HOLD):
        fail(f"--keep-service needs a hold in place ({HOLD}): without one the service hands itself to the new helper the first time it is asked")
    if os.path.exists(HOLD) and "--even-if-held" not in args and not keep_service:
        why = " ".join(open(HOLD).read().split())[:200]
        print(f"host-upgrade: nothing to do: held by {HOLD} ({why or 'no word of why'}): remove it, or --even-if-held")
        return
    machine = platform.machine()
    if machine not in MACHINES:
        fail(f"no helper is built for {machine}")
    src = os.path.join(work, "src")
    outs = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(work, "target/release/build/olo-rex/*/out")), key=os.path.getmtime)
    outs = [o for o in outs if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(o, f"olo-rex-link-{machine}"))]
    if not outs or not os.path.isdir(src):
        fail(f"no release built in {work} (tools/publish-release.sh builds it there)")
    out = outs[-1]
    carried = [open(os.path.join(out, f"olo-rex-link-{m}"), "rb").read() for m in MACHINES]
    mine = carried[MACHINES.index(machine)]
    if not mine:
        fail(f"the release's Rex carries no helper for {machine} (its build said why)")
    build = fnv(carried)
    commit = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", src, "rev-parse", "--short=9", "HEAD"], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip() or "?"
    helper = os.path.join(LINKS, f"olo-rex-link-{build}-{machine}")
    did = []

    # 1. The helper.
    new_helper = put(helper, mine, 0o755, dry)
    if new_helper:
        did.append(f"the helper {os.path.basename(helper)}")
    elif not dry:
        # (There already: still the newest by its time, the one `olo` runs.)
        newest = max(glob.glob(os.path.join(LINKS, f"olo-rex-link-*-{machine}")), key=os.path.getmtime)
        if newest != helper:
            os.utime(helper)
            did.append(f"the helper {os.path.basename(helper)} made the newest again")
    if not dry:
        r = subprocess.run([helper, "agent", "--protocol"], capture_output=True, text=True)
        if r.returncode or not r.stdout.strip().isdigit():
            fail(f"the helper put there doesn't answer ({helper} agent --protocol: {(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()})")

    # 2. The skill, and the desktop commands' own olo.
    read = lambda name: open(os.path.join(src, "apps/rex/src", name), "rb").read()
    skill, olo, lua, shim = read("bridge_skill.md"), read("bridge_olo.sh"), read("bridge_olo.lua"), read("bridge_shim.sh")
    if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(SKILL, "SKILL.md")):
        changed = [name for name, data, mode in (("SKILL.md", skill, 0o644), ("olo", olo, 0o755), ("olo.lua", lua, 0o644)) if put(os.path.join(SKILL, name), data, mode, dry)]
        if changed:
            did.append(f"the skill's {', '.join(changed)}")
    if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(COMMANDS, "olo")):
        changed = [name for name, data, mode in (("olo", olo, 0o755), ("olo.lua", lua, 0o644), ("olo-desktop-command", shim, 0o755)) if put(os.path.join(COMMANDS, name), data, mode, dry)]
        if changed:
            did.append(f"the desktop commands' {', '.join(changed)}")

    # 2b. What every coding agent on this host is told before it begins
    # (the helper's `context`: a section of Olo's in each agent's own
    # standing instructions, and the skill where Codex and Antigravity
    # look), by a helper that has it. Nothing is written that is
    # already as it should be.
    # (Only a helper that has the command is asked: one from before it
    # takes any other first word for its link's own arguments. Known by
    # the command's own usage line in the file, so nothing is run to
    # find out.)
    if not dry and b"usage: olo-rex-link context " in open(helper, "rb").read():
        r = subprocess.run([helper, "context", "install"], capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=30)
        written = [line.split(":")[0] for line in r.stdout.splitlines() if ": written (" in line]
        if r.returncode:
            print(f"host-upgrade: what agents here are told couldn't be put ({(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:200]}): the rest goes on")
        elif written:
            did.append("what agents here are told (" + ", ".join(written) + ")")

    # 3. The service.
    if dry:
        pid, exe = service(max(glob.glob(os.path.join(LINKS, f"olo-rex-link-*-{machine}")), key=os.path.getmtime))
        print(f"host-upgrade: dry run for release {commit} (helper {build}): would put " + ("; ".join(did) or "nothing") + f"; the service is pid {pid} running {os.path.basename(exe or '?')}")
        return
    handed, kept = hand_over(helper, args, keep_service)
    if handed:
        did.append(handed)
    # 4. Olo for this host's own screens, where it is here.
    desktop = os.path.join(HOME, ".local/share/olo/desktop")
    if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(desktop, "bin/olo-screen")):
        archives = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(work, f"target/dist/olo-*-{machine}.tar.zst")), key=os.path.getmtime)
        version = lambda: open(os.path.join(desktop, "share/olo/version")).read().strip() if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(desktop, "share/olo/version")) else "?"
        was = version()
        listed = subprocess.run([helper, "screen", "list"], capture_output=True, text=True)
        try:
            running = [s["name"] for s in json.loads(listed.stdout) if s.get("state") == "running"]
        except (ValueError, TypeError, KeyError):
            running = None
        if not archives:
            print(f"host-upgrade: Olo for this host's screens is left at {was}: {work}/target/dist has no archive for {machine}")
        elif running is None:
            print(f"host-upgrade: Olo for this host's screens is left at {was}: its screens couldn't be listed ({(listed.stderr or listed.stdout).strip()[-200:]})")
        elif running and "--under-screens" not in args:
            print(f"host-upgrade: Olo for this host's screens is left at {was}: {len(running)} run ({', '.join(running)}), and each would reload a moment after its programs changed; done at the next upgrade that finds none, or --under-screens")
        else:
            r = subprocess.run([helper, "screen", "install", "--from", archives[-1], "--no-chrome"] + (["--under-screens"] if "--under-screens" in args else []), capture_output=True, text=True)
            lines = [l for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l.startswith("{")]
            said = next((json.loads(l) for l in lines if '"installed"' in l and '"changed"' in l), None)
            # (Status 3: it is in, and something this host lacks is said.
            # 4: the helper's own look found a screen running, one
            # started since ours: left, and said.)
            if said is not None and said.get("held"):
                print(f"host-upgrade: Olo for this host's screens is left at {was}: {', '.join(said['held'])} started meanwhile")
            if r.returncode not in (0, 3, 4) or said is None:
                why = next((json.loads(l).get("error") for l in lines if '"error"' in l), None) or (r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[-300:]
                fail(f"Olo for this host's screens wasn't brought up to date ({why})")
            if said["changed"] or version() != was:
                did.append(f"Olo for this host's screens ({was} to {version()}: {said['changed']} programs written)")
    if not did:
        print(f"host-upgrade: nothing to do: this host has release {commit}'s helper ({build}), skill and commands, and " + (kept or f"its service is the helper's (pid {pid})"))
        return
    for line in did:
        print(f"host-upgrade: put {line}")
    if kept:
        print(f"host-upgrade: done: this host has release {commit}'s helper ({build}), skill and commands; {kept}")
        return
    print(f"host-upgrade: done: this host has release {commit}'s helper ({build}), skill and commands" + (f"; the agents service is pid {pid}" if pid else ""))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
